# tests/T03.bd - T03: BASELINE NOISE FLOOR
# =========================================================================
# PURPOSE
#   Measure the run-to-run variance of the simplest possible workload, so
#   that every later experiment in this programme has an error bar. This
#   is the control the investigation never ran: no imports, no file I/O,
#   no network, no throttle(), no branching on data, and exactly ONE
#   get_cpu() call in the whole script. It runs the same fixed arithmetic
#   burst five times per run and reports how long each one took.
#
#   Two facts in the corpus bracket the answer and they disagree:
#     - the local-only menu.bd path hit the same checkpoint at 98.6000s /
#       98.6020s / 98.6030s across three runs (3 ms over 100 s).
#     - the connected/decode path spread 40.3s -> 111.0s on identical
#       code and identical data (2.7x).
#   The hammering tests (30.4 / 30.9 / 30.5 s per round) cannot settle it:
#   those loops were throttle()-regulated, so their stability was a
#   negative-feedback controller's, not the engine's. There is no
#   controller anywhere in this file.
#
# WHAT EACH RESULT MEANS
#   (1) Tight within runs AND across runs (<5% run level, <10% sample)
#       -> engine tick delivery to a bare script is DETERMINISTIC. The
#          noise floor R is small, later A/B tests can trust small
#          differences, and menu.bd's variance comes from something this
#          probe does not do: get_cpu() polling, throttle(), a live
#          connection, file I/O, imports, or string/dict built-ins.
#   (2) Bursts scatter WITHIN each run (B0..B4 differ a lot, every run
#       scatters the same way)
#       -> ambient per-tick jitter. The engine is noisy at sample level;
#          every single-sample measurement in this programme needs
#          repeats, and the 2.7x crash-point spread may be nothing but
#          this noise accumulated over ~100 seconds of execution.
#   (3) TIGHT within each run but each run sits at its OWN level (run 3's
#       five samples all ~2.1s, run 4's all ~2.6s)
#       -> THE MOST IMPORTANT OUTCOME. Per-RUN state, not per-tick noise:
#          something about a script launch (warm-up, allocation, residual
#          load, scheduling state) sets a speed for that entire run and
#          holds it. That is exactly the shape of the central mystery -
#          same code, same data, different run, different crash point -
#          and it would move the investigation off menu.bd entirely.
#   (4) It CRASHES with "[SYSTEM] CPU overloaded"
#       -> a major result on its own: ~30 unpaced ticks is fatal. Report
#          which Bn was the last line printed. See SAFETY below.
#
# HOW TO RUN
#   1. Fresh game start preferred. Confirm nothing else is running and
#      PASTE the output of all three:  ps   /   jobs   /   daemons
#      (daemon-absence has only ever been asserted, never captured).
#   2. clear
#   3. run T03 1      <- wait for the DONE line
#      run T03 2      <- immediately, touch nothing in between
#      ... straight through to ...
#      run T03 8
#      The number only labels the output so pasted blocks cannot be
#      mislabelled or reordered; it changes nothing the script does.
#      Foreground only - never `run T03 1 &`.
#   4. Between runs: no alt-tab, no menus, no clear, no other typing. The
#      only keystrokes are the next run command.
#   5. Paste all 8 blocks (7 lines each) in order, plus the wall-clock
#      time of day at run 1 and at run 8.
#
# REPEATS
#   8 back to back. ABSOLUTE MINIMUM 6 - fewer cannot distinguish a 15%
#   spread from chance. If one run looks wild, add 4 MORE runs; never
#   discard it. Re-run the whole block from scratch if the game is
#   restarted, because this block is the reference dataset that T14-T19
#   and T20-T25 are judged against.
#   ~30 s per run at base tier, ~5 minutes for the whole block.
#
# FIRST THING TO CHECK IN THE OUTPUT
#   B0's burst= value. Decimals (2.4731) mean the clock has sub-second
#   resolution and burst= is the primary scalar - which is what
#   field_notes' +98.6020s figures imply, even though web_api.txt
#   declares get_unix_time() -> int. If every burst= prints as a whole
#   number (2 / 2.0 / 3), say so immediately: the clock is integer
#   resolution, burst= is useless at this scale, DONE total= becomes the
#   only scalar, and that documented contradiction resolves for the docs.
#
# SAFETY - THE BURST *IS* THE EXPERIMENT, SO IT IS DELIBERATELY UNPACED
#   The burst is 10 iterations of a 2-line body = 20 executed body lines,
#   plus up to 11 loop-condition evaluations, plus the 3 lines around it:
#   23-34 ticks, i.e. 2.3-3.4 s at base tier (10 ticks/s) and less on
#   every higher tier. The kill needs 5 STRAIGHT seconds at 100%, and
#   test_cpu_decay.bd's fatal burst was 50 iterations (~100-150 ticks),
#   so this should keep ~1.6 s of margin even in the worst case. If T02
#   measured the tier as base AND a burst here reads over 3.5 s, change
#   ITER from 10 to 6 BEFORE run 1 - never mid-block, or the 8 runs stop
#   being comparable. Everything else is paced: every burst is preceded
#   by a full sleep(1) and the prelude's own sleep(1), identically.
#
# WHY THERE IS ONLY ONE get_cpu() CALL
#   Every prior test used get_cpu() as controller (inside throttle()), as
#   instrument (in every log line) and as suspect (it may add load) all
#   at once, so no observed value could be attributed to the workload
#   rather than to the measurement. Here it runs exactly once, on the
#   first executed line, BEFORE t0 is taken - outside every timed window
#   and an identical constant in every run. This also makes run N+1's
#   cpu0 a clean carryover reading for run N: test_cpu_decay.bd got
#   exactly 0 on a fresh script, so anything above 0 here is load that
#   existed before this script's first line ran.
#
# @param run "run number, 1-8 - only used to label the output" default="?"

var RUN = get_param("run", "?")
var ITER = 10
var c0 = get_cpu()
var t0 = get_unix_time()
var n = 0
var j = 0
var x = 0
var a = 0
var b = 0
log("START run=" + str(RUN) + " cpu0=" + str(c0) + " iter=" + str(ITER))
sleep(1)

while n < 5:
    sleep(1)
    j = 0
    a = get_unix_time()
    while j < ITER:
        x = j * 2
        j = j + 1
    b = get_unix_time()
    sleep(1)
    log("R" + str(RUN) + " B" + str(n) + " burst=" + str(b - a) + " total=" + str(b - t0))
    n = n + 1

log("R" + str(RUN) + " DONE total=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
